WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.169
INTRO
Over the past few years, you might’ve heard

00:00:02.169 --> 00:00:05.910
about the push to delist chinese companies
from the US stock market if they don’t comply

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.149
with US auditing rules.

00:00:08.149 --> 00:00:13.340
Initially, this might seem like more of a
political move rather than an objective move,

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.890
but a lot of evidence would actually suggest
that this move is indeed warranted.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:23.500
One of these examples is a Chinese Starbucks
copycat called Luckin Coffee.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:28.170
Just a few years ago, Luckin Coffee was a
booming startup within China that had seemingly

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.619
unlocked the key to Chinese coffee lovers.

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:34.040
But all of this would come crumbling down
when it was revealed that Luckin was faking

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:43.410
hundreds of millions of dollars worth of sales.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:48.760
When the news broke, Luckin Coffee plummeted
over 97% which wiped out basically every dollar

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:49.880
invested into the company.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:54.350
So, here’s how Luckin Coffee went from trying
to dethrone Starbucks to being delisted from

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.300
the American stock market.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:04.050
LU ZHENGYAO
Taking a look back, the entire fiasco circles

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.250
back to a man named Lu Zhengyao.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.870
Lu started off his career as a government
bureaucrat who served in the Hebei province

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:11.540
located just outside of Beijing.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:15.370
But, it didn’t take him long to switch over
to the private industry in the early 1990s

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:19.260
when he saw technology companies blowing up
leading up to the dotcom bubble.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:23.230
Lu decided to jump in on the action by selling
various parts within the telecommunications

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:24.230
industry.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:27.600
Once the dotcom bubble burst, Lu jumped over
to the rapidly growing Chinese auto industry

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:28.950
by selling insurance.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:33.130
And in 2007, he would open his own car rental
company called Car Inc.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:36.390
Lu was able to raise hundreds of millions
from foreign investors and use that money

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:38.920
to offer car rental prices that were unheard
of.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.570
Car inc was barely making money given that
they were renting out these cars for basically

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:46.970
0 profit, but given that they were undercutting
all the competition, it didn’t take long

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.550
for Car to build up a massive customer base.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:54.670
By 2013, Car was the largest rental provider
in China and their biggest competitor Hertz

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:58.990
would actually give in by purchasing a 20%
stake in the company and merging their operations

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:00.080
with Car.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.700
But just 2 years later, Hertz dumped most
of their position in Car, so maybe they knew

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.670
something about Lu’s business practices.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:12.060
But at the time, this was simply written off
as a financial move as Hertz was losing hundreds

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.120
of millions themselves.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.450
In the meantime, Lu was praised for being
a masterful businessman who had the midas

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:18.450
touch.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.750
He had succeeded in the telecommunications,
insurance, and rental industries, and his

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.030
timing was impeccable.

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:26.840
He knew exactly when to get in and exactly
when to get out.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:31.121
So, when Lu started talking about his vision
for a Starbucks like coffee shop, investors

00:02:31.121 --> 00:02:32.930
were sold.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:39.790
LUCKIN COFFEE
You see, Starbucks had been trying to break

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:43.630
into the Chinese market for nearly two decades
at that point, and though they were able to

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:48.220
grow a pretty sizable operation, they struggled
to match their success in western countries.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.920
This wasn’t necessarily Starbucks fault
though, the problem was that coffee simply

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:53.430
wasn’t that popular in China.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:59.250
As of 2018, annual per capita coffee consumption
came in at 388 cups in the US.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:03.970
In China, it was just 6 cups or 64 times less.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:07.080
Starbucks was well aware of this, and they
tried to address this disparity by selling

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.450
the social aspect of their shops.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.080
But Lu would take the exact opposite approach.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.989
Lu told the media that he and his colleagues
had become coffee lovers as they put in long

00:03:14.989 --> 00:03:16.620
days trying to grow Car Inc.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.270
He suggested that coffee was the perfect boost
that the Chinese needed to get through long

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:21.270
work days.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:24.580
And he felt that the only reason coffee hadn’t
caught on in China was because of its lack

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.010
of affordability as opposed to a lack of demand.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:31.330
Starbucks hadn’t really reduced their prices
when they moved over to China with a grande

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.910
latte selling for $4.57 or 32 yuan.

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:39.170
In 2017, the average Chinese citizen was only
making about 36,000 yuan.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:44.370
So, buying a grande latte was equivalent to
an American spending $31 on a cup of coffee.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:47.849
Lu suggested that he could fix this issue
by embracing a new coffee shop model based

00:03:47.849 --> 00:03:49.840
on kiosks and automation.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.530
Unlike Starbucks locations, Luckin locations
had very little seating if any.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:57.230
They completely eliminated the social aspect
and simply focused on reducing cost as much

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:58.400
as possible.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.270
Luckin also required that customers place
their orders on the Luckin app which eliminated

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.700
the need for cashiers and minimized labor
costs.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:08.171
Lu was hoping to eventually switch to smart
vending machines that would completely eliminate

00:04:08.171 --> 00:04:12.120
the need for employees altogether, but he
quickly ran into a brick wall.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:15.750
The truth was shrinking the stores and reducing
labor costs wasn’t even close to making

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.169
up for the revenue disparity.

00:04:17.169 --> 00:04:22.370
In 2018, operating expenses were nearly triple
sales meaning that Luckin spent nearly $3

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:24.159
for every $1 of revenue.

00:04:24.159 --> 00:04:27.789
To make things worse, it didn’t take long
for Starbucks to retaliate.

00:04:27.789 --> 00:04:33.289
FIGHTING STARBUCKS
In 2018, Starbucks formed a partnership with

00:04:33.289 --> 00:04:34.289
Alibaba.

00:04:34.289 --> 00:04:37.999
Alibaba already had an established delivery
network which Starbucks could leverage.

00:04:37.999 --> 00:04:41.659
This allowed Starbucks to set up their own
kiosk type stores called Starbucks Now with

00:04:41.659 --> 00:04:43.259
minimal space and employees.

00:04:43.259 --> 00:04:47.759
This significantly increased the pressure
on Luckin Coffee, but Lu was still confident

00:04:47.759 --> 00:04:50.930
that he just had to sustain the losses for
long enough to achieve incredible economies

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:52.719
of scale and become profitable.

00:04:52.719 --> 00:04:57.430
But to sustain such losses, he needed the
help of foreign investors, so Lu hired an

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:59.629
investment banker named Reinout Schakel.

00:04:59.629 --> 00:05:03.719
Reinout was appointed as the CFO of Luckin
Coffee and he was the only non-Chinese executive

00:05:03.719 --> 00:05:04.860
at the company.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.069
Fortunately for Luckin Coffee, Reinout was
able to open some big doors in terms of funding.

00:05:09.069 --> 00:05:13.779
By the end of 2018, Luckin was able to raise
a total of $400 million with one of their

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:16.729
most notable investors being Singapore's Sovereign
wealth fund.

00:05:16.729 --> 00:05:20.460
With this influx of capital, Lu was able to
retaliate against Starbucks partnership with

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:24.919
Alibaba by forming a partnership with Alibaba’s
biggest rival Tencent.

00:05:24.919 --> 00:05:29.159
Alibaba might’ve been able to circumvent
Luckin’s kiosk model, but Alibaba had no

00:05:29.159 --> 00:05:31.360
chance at circumventing Tencent’s order
flow.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.220
Tencent is the owner of WeChat which is a
super app that is capable of doing everything

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.340
from social media to mobile payments.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:42.990
WeChat is ubiquitous in China boasting a total
of 1.2 billion active monthly users.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:46.219
Luckin could easily advertise coupons and
discounts and promote Luckin Coffee to basically

00:05:46.219 --> 00:05:48.339
the entirety of China through Tencent.

00:05:48.339 --> 00:05:51.190
This was a massive deal and got major investors
excited.

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:56.499
In April of 2019 for instance, Luckin raised
$150 million, $125 million of which came from

00:05:56.499 --> 00:05:57.499
BlackRock.

00:05:57.499 --> 00:06:01.000
But the best way to raise money was of course
through the irrational public markets.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.909
So, Luckin started working with American banks
to take Luckin public.

00:06:04.909 --> 00:06:09.259
Bank of America was selected to be one of
the underwriters of the IPO, but Bank of America

00:06:09.259 --> 00:06:12.840
eventually backed out after Luckin executives
demanded for a higher valuation than what

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:14.520
Bank of America gave them.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:18.559
Nonetheless, Luckin was able to complete their
IPO in May of 2019 with the help of Morgan

00:06:18.559 --> 00:06:20.520
Stanley and Credit Suisse.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:24.970
Luckin debuted at $17 per share, but it would
quickly rocket to $25 per share by the end

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:28.880
of the first trading day which valued the
company at $5.88 billion.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:31.389
By the start of 2020, Luckin was killing it.

00:06:31.389 --> 00:06:33.789
They had overtaken Starbucks in terms of locations.

00:06:33.789 --> 00:06:38.149
At the time, Starbucks had 3600 locations
while Luckin had 4500.

00:06:38.149 --> 00:06:41.590
And Luckin’s quarterly sales growth was
through the roof.

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:45.410
In late 2019, Luckin reported a quarterly
sales growth of 6x.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:50.189
It seemed like Lu had done it again and investors
piled in confident that they were investing

00:06:50.189 --> 00:06:51.379
into the next Starbucks.

00:06:51.379 --> 00:06:56.039
By January of 2020, Luckin Coffee stock had
grown to $50 per share valuing the company

00:06:56.039 --> 00:06:58.030
at $11.8 billion.

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:01.810
To many investors even this was a great price
to jump in.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.610
After all, Starbucks is worth $137 billion.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:09.400
So, even if Luckin was able to grow to half
the size of Starbucks, that would be a 5x

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:10.400
investment.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.800
And, this likely would’ve been the case
if Luckin wasn’t lying.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:18.789
As Luckin grew a massive investor base, they
also built a massive group of skeptics.

00:07:18.789 --> 00:07:23.360
TOO GOOD TO BE TRUE
To many investors, Luckin’s growth numbers

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:25.259
were simply way too good to be true.

00:07:25.259 --> 00:07:29.639
I mean what type of company grows their revenue
6 fold in a single quarter.

00:07:29.639 --> 00:07:33.319
One of these skeptics was a man named Carson
Block who was the founder of Muddy Waters

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:34.349
Research.

00:07:34.349 --> 00:07:37.990
Carson had correctly exposed another fraudulent
Chinese company called Sino Forest Corporation

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:41.520
a decade ago, and he sensed that Luckin was
in a similar boat.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:45.830
But he knew that exposing a company as large
as Luckin would be a massive undertaking financially.

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:49.719
It would cost an absurd amount of money to
monitor thousands of stores and search for

00:07:49.719 --> 00:07:50.719
disparities.

00:07:50.719 --> 00:07:55.029
So, Carson didn’t even try to expose Luckin,
but someone else did.

00:07:55.029 --> 00:07:58.939
An anonymous fund manager had been monitoring
Luckin Coffee for a while and had already

00:07:58.939 --> 00:08:01.039
uncovered massive disparities.

00:08:01.039 --> 00:08:05.319
The fund manager had hired 1000 investigators
to monitor the foot traffic at Luckin locations

00:08:05.319 --> 00:08:06.520
across China.

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:10.180
These investigators also collected 25,000
receipts from customers to estimate the average

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:11.849
cost of each order.

00:08:11.849 --> 00:08:15.189
Using this data, the fund was able to estimate
the quarterly sales of Luckin throughout the

00:08:15.189 --> 00:08:17.419
third and fourth quarters of 2019.

00:08:17.419 --> 00:08:21.619
And the disparity between the estimated sales
and reported sales wasn’t 5 or 10%.

00:08:21.619 --> 00:08:26.089
No, it was 69% in the 3rd quarter and 88%
in the fourth quarter.

00:08:26.089 --> 00:08:30.620
It was pretty clear that the Luckin was faking
sales, but the fund manager didn't want to

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:34.380
release the evidence himself as he feared
the backlash he would receive from investors.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.750
So, he asked Carson to release the report,
and Carson would follow through.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:42.560
As soon as the report debuted in February
of 2020, Luckin Coffee plunged.

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:46.061
Luckin Coffee of course refused the allegations
and many investors refused to believe it.

00:08:46.061 --> 00:08:51.360
But, it didn’t take long for the SEC to
get involved, and once the SEC got involved,

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:54.270
Luckin really had no choice but to come clean.

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:57.960
They could’ve waited until the SEC exposed
them which would probably take quite some

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:00.340
time given how slow the SEC is.

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:04.880
But, when the SEC did finally expose them,
there would be no coming back for Luckin as

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:08.380
the SEC’s findings would completely discredit
the entire executive team.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:12.490
So, Luckin’s executives decided to pin the
blame on a couple of executives and hopefully

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:13.490
save face.

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:17.620
On April 2, 2020, Luckin admitted that their
numbers were overstated.

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:21.690
They tried to claim that the fraud was carried
out by two executives named Jenny Qian and

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:24.710
Jian Liu, but that the rest of the company
was clean.

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:28.110
Investors didn’t buy that story and Luckin
Coffee stock would jump into the abyss.

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:31.820
In May, Nasdaq announced that they would be
delisting Luckin Coffee from the stock market

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:33.280
at the end of June.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:37.030
And unsurprisingly, Luckin Coffee would plummet
to less than a $1 per share.

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:41.930
In July, Luckin Coffee confirmed that $300
million worth of sales was fabricated and

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:44.210
Lu would be ousted that same month.

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:47.710
CNBC estimates that throughout the duration
of the fraud, Luckin was able to raise a total

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:52.250
of $864 million which basically evaporated
after they were exposed.

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:54.750
And most positions ended up looking like this.

00:09:54.750 --> 00:10:00.060
THE AFTERMATH
As for Lu, he claims that he was never trying

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:04.400
to deceive investors and that he was just
trying to grow Luckin as fast as possible.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.950
And I do think he’s partially telling the
truth.

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:08.910
It’s not like he was trying to run a pump
and dump.

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:13.110
He was actually trying to grow a long term
company, but he succumbed to external pressures

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:15.040
and turned to faking it till he made it.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:17.590
And unfortunately for Lu, he never made it.

00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:21.980
It should be noted though that Luckin Coffee
never stopped operations and many bag holders

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:25.760
still think Luckin can make a comeback, but
only time will tell.

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:29.200
Do you guys think Luckin can make a comeback
or do you think that the company should be

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:30.730
shut down for their deception?

